WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.036 --> 00:00:20.868
At eight o'clock the next morning, we released the small boat from its hiding place atop the Nautilus. The sea was fairly calm.

2
00:00:20.877 --> 00:00:27.273
With Conseil and me rowing, Ned steered us safely past the coral reef that surrounded the island of Gilboa.

3
00:00:27.274 --> 00:00:30.614
I was overwhelmed at setting foot on land again.

4
00:00:30.615 --> 00:00:36.019
Ned strode across the sand as if he were an explorer taking possession of the island.

5
00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:43.424
It was hard to believe that only two months had passed since we had become "passengers onboard the Nautilus" in Captain Nemo's words.

6
00:00:43.425 --> 00:00:47.686
In reality we were prisoners of the submarine's commander.

7
00:00:47.687 --> 00:00:51.090
Within a few minutes we had plunged into a deep forest.

8
00:00:51.091 --> 00:00:58.067
While I was impressed by the beautiful mimosa and hibiscus flowers, Ned concentrated on getting something to eat.

9
00:00:58.068 --> 00:01:02.583
He discovered a coconut palm and retrieved some of the fruit.

10
00:01:02.584 --> 00:01:08.424
After he broke open a coconut with his hatchet, we drank the milk and ate the meat with gusto.

11
00:01:08.425 --> 00:01:10.750
"Excellent!" proclaimed Ned.

12
00:01:10.751 --> 00:01:13.329
"Exquisite!" said Conseil.

13
00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:21.067
"Professor, I don't think that Captain Nemo would object to our bringing some coconuts onboard the Nautilus, do you?" said Ned.

14
00:01:21.068 --> 00:01:26.718
"I don't think he'd mind," I said, "but he won't taste them because they come from the land."

15
00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:29.392
"Too bad for him," said Conseil.

16
00:01:29.393 --> 00:01:34.392
"And all the better for us because there will be more for us to eat," said Ned.

17
00:01:34.393 --> 00:01:39.884
"I have only one note of caution," I said as Ned began to attack another coconut tree.

18
00:01:39.885 --> 00:01:48.709
"Coconuts are delicious, but before filling our boat with them, let's explore and see if this island produces any other useful things.

19
00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:52.868
Fresh vegetables would be a welcome addition to our diet onboard the Nautilus."

20
00:01:52.869 --> 00:02:01.940
"You are right," said Conseil. "I propose that we save three spaces on our little boat: one for fruits, one for vegetables, and one for meat.

21
00:02:01.941 --> 00:02:07.702
And speaking of meat, I have yet to see even the smallest example of an animal on this island."

22
00:02:07.703 --> 00:02:10.877
"Conseil, you must be patient," said Ned.

23
00:02:10.878 --> 00:02:16.686
For two hours we walked through the forest until we came upon a grove of breadfruit trees.

24
00:02:16.687 --> 00:02:21.615
Ned was familiar with the breadfruit, having eaten it during his numerous voyages.

25
00:02:21.616 --> 00:02:24.187
He became very excited at the sight of it.

26
00:02:24.188 --> 00:02:30.424
"Professor, I will die if I don't taste some of this breadfruit right now," said Ned.

27
00:02:30.425 --> 00:02:36.829
"Taste as much as you want," I said. "We're here to explore the island and the food it provides."

28
00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:39.575
"It won't take long," Ned promised.

29
00:02:39.576 --> 00:02:43.821
While Conseil and I gathered breadfruit, Ned got a fire going.

30
00:02:43.822 --> 00:02:49.083
Conseil brought a dozen breadfruit to Ned, who cut them into thick slices.

31
00:02:49.084 --> 00:02:54.646
"You will see how good this is, Professor," said Ned. "Have you ever eaten it before?"

32
00:02:54.647 --> 00:02:58.059
"No, Ned." I wasn't sure that I would like it.

33
00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:06.067
"Well then, prepare yourself for something delicious. If you don't come back for more, then I'm no longer the king of the harpooners."

34
00:03:06.068 --> 00:03:11.242
After a few minutes, the part of the fruit that was exposed to the fire was completely roasted.

35
00:03:11.243 --> 00:03:16.884
The interior looked like a white pastry, and I must confess that Ned was right.

36
00:03:16.885 --> 00:03:22.138
It was absolutely delicious, and I ate it with great enthusiasm.

37
00:03:22.139 --> 00:03:28.464
We continued to travel through the forest, gathering bananas, pineapples, beans, and yams.

38
00:03:28.465 --> 00:03:32.956
At five o'clock we loaded our small boat and rowed away from shore.

39
00:03:32.957 --> 00:03:37.218
As we pulled up next to the Nautilus, no one came to greet us.

40
00:03:37.219 --> 00:03:44.964
The black hull seemed deserted. We loaded our provisions onboard and returned to our cabins, where we found supper waiting.

41
00:03:44.965 --> 00:03:49.535
The next morning there was nothing new onboard, no sign of life.

42
00:03:49.536 --> 00:03:53.456
Our small boat rested where we had left it next to the submarine.

43
00:03:53.457 --> 00:04:01.535
Again we rowed out to the island. Ned hoped to have better luck with regard to hunting, so we decided to visit another part of the island.

44
00:04:01.536 --> 00:04:07.765
After crossing a large prairie, we came to a small forest filled with brightly colored birds.

45
00:04:07.766 --> 00:04:12.932
"These are only small birds," said Conseil in a disappointed tone.

46
00:04:12.933 --> 00:04:17.353
"Yes, but they can be eaten if you're hungry enough," said Ned.

47
00:04:17.354 --> 00:04:23.257
We decided to keep walking and soon came across some larger, more beautiful birds.

48
00:04:23.258 --> 00:04:29.099
"Birds of paradise!" I exclaimed as the birds took off in undulating flight.

49
00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:35.995
We must have used half our ammunition trying to bring one down, but we were unsuccessful. Hunger drove us on.

50
00:04:35.996 --> 00:04:42.987
Finally Conseil shot two pigeons for our breakfast. While the birds roasted, Ned prepared some breadfruit.

51
00:04:42.988 --> 00:04:50.138
"These birds are excellent," he said, "but they are only side dishes. I won't be happy until I get some cutlets."

52
00:04:50.139 --> 00:04:56.566
I knew that I wouldn't be happy until I got a bird of paradise—not to eat but to keep for my collection.

53
00:04:56.567 --> 00:04:59.733
After eating we continued walking.

54
00:04:59.734 --> 00:05:07.812
Some harmless snakes glided out of our path. Birds of paradise fled at our approach and I despaired at ever getting one.

55
00:05:07.813 --> 00:05:15.884
But Conseil, who was walking ahead of me, suddenly uttered a cry of triumph and held up a magnificent specimen.

56
00:05:15.885 --> 00:05:24.543
"Bravo, Conseil!" I cried. "If only I could bring this bird back to Paris and display it at the zoo in the botanical garden.

57
00:05:24.544 --> 00:05:27.114
How did you ever manage to catch it?"

58
00:05:27.115 --> 00:05:31.289
"If you examine the bird, you will see that I don't deserve any credit.

59
00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:37.210
This bird has been eating nutmeg, which has made it quite drunk and easy to catch."

60
00:05:37.211 --> 00:05:42.368
No sooner did I get my wish, than Ned got his: a large hog.

61
00:05:42.369 --> 00:05:48.439
We spent the rest of the day exploring the island, and Ned also managed to shoot some small kangaroos.

62
00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:51.955
By six o'clock we were back at the beach.

63
00:05:51.956 --> 00:05:59.614
Our small boat was still there and the Nautilus looked like a long rock emerging from the waves two miles away.

64
00:05:59.615 --> 00:06:03.178
Ned quickly got down to the business of preparing supper.

65
00:06:03.179 --> 00:06:08.599
The hog, grilling on the coals, soon scented the air with a delicious smell.

66
00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:17.829
We had an excellent supper. Besides the hog, we dined on pigeons, breadfruit, mangoes, pineapples, and coconut milk.

67
00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:22.154
"Suppose we don't return to the Nautilus tonight?" said Conseil.

68
00:06:22.155 --> 00:06:25.313
"Suppose we never return?" added Ned.

69
00:06:25.314 --> 00:06:30.298
Just then a stone fell at our feet and cut short the conversation.

70
00:06:30.299 --> 00:06:33.908
My hand stopped in the midst of putting food into my mouth.

71
00:06:33.909 --> 00:06:39.313
We scanned the edge of the forest without moving but didn't see anything.

72
00:06:39.314 --> 00:06:45.226
"Stones don't fall from the sky unless they're meteorites," observed Conseil.

73
00:06:45.227 --> 00:06:50.130
A second stone knocked the pigeon's leg from Conseil's hand, proving him right.

74
00:06:50.131 --> 00:06:54.630
All three of us stood up and shouldered our guns, ready to answer any attack.

75
00:06:54.631 --> 00:06:58.138
"Maybe it was just an ape?" suggested Ned.

76
00:06:58.139 --> 00:07:03.051
"No, look." Conseil pointed toward the woods. "Natives."

77
00:07:03.052 --> 00:07:09.455
About twenty natives, armed with slingshots and bows and arrows, appeared on the edge of the woods.

78
00:07:09.456 --> 00:07:12.471
They were only about a hundred feet from us.

79
00:07:12.472 --> 00:07:14.368
"To the boat!" I cried.

80
00:07:14.369 --> 00:07:23.528
Our boat was moored about sixty feet away. We started running as fast as we could while stones and arrows fell thickly around us.
